/*
========================================
        withContext() — COMPLETE SUMMARY
========================================

Definition:
withContext() is a suspending function that
switches coroutine context for a block of code
without creating a new coroutine.

----------------------------------------
WHAT withContext() CHANGES
----------------------------------------

1) Dispatcher  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(Dispatchers.IO) {
       // Runs under IO dispatcher
   }

   ✔ Execution responsibility moves to IO dispatcher
   ✔ Scheduler decides which thread to use
   ❗ Thread switching is NOT guaranteed


2) CoroutineName  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(CoroutineName("MyTask")) {
       // Coroutine name becomes "MyTask"
   }

   ✔ Useful for debugging


3) CoroutineExceptionHandler  ✅ (If Provided)

   withContext(myExceptionHandler) {
       // New handler used inside this block
   }

   ✔ Overrides parent handler inside this block


----------------------------------------
WHAT withContext() DOES NOT CHANGE
----------------------------------------

1) Job  ❌ (Does NOT change by default)

   ✔ Same Job is preserved
   ✔ Structured concurrency maintained
   ✔ Cancellation propagates properly
   ✔ Not a new coroutine

   NOTE:
   It CAN change only if you explicitly pass a new Job
   (rare and usually not recommended)


2) It does NOT create a new coroutine  ❌

   launch { }  → Creates new coroutine
   async { }   → Creates new coroutine
   withContext { } → Suspends current coroutine

   ✔ It pauses current coroutine
   ✔ Executes block under new context
   ✔ Resumes original coroutine after completion


3) Thread is NOT guaranteed to change  ❌

   Dispatcher switch ≠ Thread switch

   Thread changes only if:
   - Target dispatcher uses different thread pool
   - Scheduler decides to move it

   Example:
   Default → IO may use same worker thread
   Main → IO must switch thread


----------------------------------------
HOW IT WORKS INTERNALLY
----------------------------------------

Step 1: Suspend current coroutine
Step 2: Create new context (Current + New)
Step 3: Dispatcher resumes coroutine
Step 4: Execute block
Step 5: Resume back to original context


----------------------------------------
MENTAL MODEL
----------------------------------------

withContext(...) means:

    "Temporarily change execution environment
     of THIS coroutine, not create a new one."


----------------------------------------
FINAL INTERVIEW ONE-LINER
----------------------------------------

withContext switches coroutine context
(dispatcher, name, exception handler if provided),
keeps the same Job,
does not create a new coroutine,
and does not guarantee thread switching.
*/



Table
*****
| Element                | Changes?            |
| ---------------------- | ------------------- |
| Dispatcher             | ✅ Yes (if provided) |
| Job                    | ❌ No (same Job)     |
| Coroutine Name         | ✅ If provided       |
| Exception Handler      | ✅ If provided       |
| Thread                 | ❌ Not guaranteed    |
| New Coroutine Created? | ❌ No                |
